<a href="#"><img alt="P1" src="https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/p1.svg?v=9" align="top"></a> <a href="#"><img alt="security" src="https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/Security.svg?v=2" align="top"></a> **Redirects can leak runner keys**

`HttpRunnerClient.start_run` now probes capabilities through `get_gateway_caps` when the effort is `max`. That probe uses plain `urllib.request.urlopen`, bypassing the runner's `_NoRedirect` protection. If `/v1/capabilities` redirects to another host, urllib forwards the runner's API key there. Use the runner's redirect-blocking transport for this probe.

**How this was verified:** The new call passes the runner key into a redirect-following request, and urllib copies `Authorization` into the redirected request without checking the destination host.

**Knowledge Base Used:** [Agent runtime and gateway](https://app.greptile.com/hermes-webui/-/custom-context/knowledge-base/nesquena/hermes-webui/-/docs/agent-runtime-and-gateway.md)
--- 4270595874 greptile-apps[bot] 2026-10-14T12:14:11Z api/routes.py 2245d7666e in_reply_to None
<a href="#"><img alt="P1" src="https://greptile-static-assets.s3.amazonaws.com/badges/p1.svg?v=9" align="top"></a> **Wakeups use another profile's effort**

`_start_run` reads `get_config()` outside the session's profile scope. Automatic process and delegation wakeups run on fresh threads without that scope, and `_server_initiated_turn_routing` restores the previous scope before this read. A named-profile session therefore receives the process profile's reasoning effort instead of its own. For example, a process profile set to `none` disables reasoning for a session whose profile requests `high`.

Resolve the effort and model capabilities inside the owning session's profile scope.

**Knowledge Base Used:**
- [Chat and agent execution](https://app.greptile.com/hermes-webui/-/custom-context/knowledge-base/nesquena/hermes-webui/-/docs/chat-and-agent-execution.md)
